e and average discount side by side, with the month-on-month % change in booked revenue and in contribution."}, {"id": "S2", "text": "Using the contribution-margin convention above (or your own, clearly stated), compute revenue (booked order value) and contribution margin per month. Return the months where revenue rose but contribution fell versus the previous month, using LAG()."}, {"id": "S6", "text": "Using a window function, find each category's single best and single worst month by contribution, in one result set."}, {"id": "S9", "text": "Per Acquisition Channel: orders, ad spend, delivered GMV, ROAS (GMV / ad spend) and contribution before and after ad spend. Which channel burns the most money?"}, {"id": "S1", "text": "Rank the 20 listing managers by delivered GMV (sum of order_value on Delivered lines). Return name, category, marketplace, GMV and rank."}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Order Lookup", "tasks": [{"id": "D3", "text": "Add a date-range filter on Order Date and a separate date-range filter on Delivery Date, and make sure both can be applied together."}, {"id": "D1", "text": "Build a search box that takes an Order ID and shows the full story of that one line: product and listing manager, the price chain (MRP -> discount -> selling price -> ex-GST revenue), the cost stack (COGS, fee, shipping, packaging, ad spend, return costs) and the resulting contribution as a waterfall."}, {"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for Marketplace, Category, Campaign, Listing Manager and Order Status that combine with the date filters (AND logic) rather than replacing them."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "On the same screen show customer context: new vs repeat, city and state, acquisition channel, payment mode, review rating and comment, and any remarks."}, {"id": "D5", "text": "Make the order-results table itself filterable and sortable by any visible column, so someone can browse instead of only searching by exact Order ID."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "tit