select id, 'KOJOH-DDT-LIC-0092', 'standard', $pack${"phases": [{"key": "python", "title": "Phase 1, Python: Clean the Data", "tasks": [{"id": "P6", "text": "Normalise Discount % (`40%`, `40.00`, `0.4000`, `40.0 %`), Quantity (`2`, `2 pcs`, `Two`), and Pincode (`226010`, `226010.0`, `226 010`). Then recompute discount from MRP and Selling Price, confirm it matches the stated discount, and list the rows where it does not."}, {"id": "P3", "text": "Write a parser for the money text formats found across the ten money columns (plain, western comma, `Rs` + Indian comma, rupee symbol, space-padded, trailing-decimal, and rare `k` notation) and apply it everywhere. Flag the rows with negative selling prices and the rows where MRP and Selling Price were swapped."}, {"id": "P5", "text": "Standardise Marketplace, Category, Payment Mode, Acquisition Channel, City and State spellings, and collapse Order Status (about 25 spellings) into Delivered / Returned / Cancelled / RTO."}, {"id": "P1", "text": "Load the raw CSV/XLSX and profile it: row count, column count, null counts per column, and a written note on the five things that look most wrong before you touch anything."}, {"id": "P2", "text": "Remove duplicate order entries (~190 of them). State your de-duplication rule and how many genuine order lines remain."}, {"id": "P7", "text": "Resolve every Listing Manager entry to one of 20 distinct people. Each person appears under roughly 8 spellings, and Emp Code (blank on ~5% of rows, typo'd or lowercased on more) only partly helps. Document your method. It is graded on defensibility, not just the final count."}, {"id": "P4", "text": "Write a parser for the five date formats across the three date columns, including Excel serial numbers (days since 1899-12-30). Derive a clean order month from Order Date (the ERP's own Month column is ~40% blank) and flag every row where the delivery date falls before the order date."}, {"id": "P8", "text": "Customer ID is blank on ~4% of rows and the system's New/Repeat flag is wrong on ~5%. Rebuild a trustworthy customer key (name variants + city + pincode) and re-derive New vs Repeat from the order history instead of trusting the flag. State how many customers you end up with."}]}, {"key": "sql", "title": "Phase 2, SQL: Answer the Business Questions", "tasks": [{"id": "S4", "text": "Build a category x month table of contribution margin %. Flag every cell that is negative and say which category/month combinations are responsible for the loss."}, {"id": "S5", "text": "Group order lines into discount bands (0-20%, 20-25%, 25-30%, 30-35%, 35-40%, 40-45%, 45%+). For each band return line count, return rate and average contribution per line. At what discount does the average line stop making money?"}, {"id": "S11", "text": "Customer lifetime value: total contribution per customer across all their orders, averaged by acquisition cohort (customers whose first order was a Diwali line at 35%+ off vs everyone else), plus the number of customers in each cohort."}, {"id": "S9", "text": "Per Acquisition Channel: orders, ad spend, delivered GMV, ROAS (GMV / ad spend) and contribution before and after ad spend. Which channel burns the most money?"}, {"id": "S7", "text": "Compare Payment Mode COD vs prepaid: for each, the share of lines that end Returned, RTO or Cancelled, and the rupee cost of that leakage (shipping + pickup + packaging + write-offs)."}, {"id": "S8", "text": "For September, October and November only, return booked revenue, delivered revenue, contribution, return rate and average discount side by side, with the month-on-month % change in booked revenue and in contribution."}, {"id": "S10", "text": "Cohort query: classify each customer by the discount on their FIRST order, then compute the share who placed a second order within 90 days. Use a fixed 90-day window and exclude customers acquired after 31 Dec 2025 so the comparison is fair."}, {"id": "S1", "text": "Rank the 20 listing managers by delivered GMV (sum of order_value on Delivered lines). Return name, category, marketplace, GMV and rank."}, {"id": "S6", "text": "Using a window function, find each category's single best and single worst month by contribution, in one result set."}, {"id": "S3", "text": "Compute total contribution per listing manager and rank them. Compare this rank with S1's rank and return everyone who moved by more than 5 places."}, {"id": "S2", "text": "Using the contribution-margin convention above (or your own, clearly stated), compute revenue (booked order value) and contribution margin per month. Return the months where revenue rose but contribution fell versus the previous month, using LAG()."}, {"id": "S12", "text": "Using a CTE, build a 3-stage funnel per marketplace: orders placed -> orders kept (Delivered and not returned) -> customers who reordered within 90 days of that delivery."}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Order Lookup", "tasks": [{"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for Marketplace, Category, Campaign, Listing Manager and Order Status that combine with the date filters (AND logic) rather than replacing them."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "On the same screen show customer context: new vs repeat, city and state, acquisition channel, payment mode, review rating and comment, and any remarks."}, {"id": "D1", "text": "Build a search box that takes an Order ID and shows the full story of that one line: product and listing manager, the price chain (MRP -> discount -> selling price -> ex-GST revenue), the cost stack (COGS, fee, shipping, packaging, ad spend, return costs) and the resulting contribution as a waterfall."}, {"id": "D3", "text": "Add a date-range filter on Order Date and a separate date-range filter on Delivery Date, and make sure both can be applied together."}, {"id": "D5", "text": "Make the order-results table itself filterable and sortable by any visible column, so someone can browse instead of only searching by exact Order ID."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "title": "Phase 3b, Dashboard Page 2: Analytics", "tasks": [{"id": "D14", "text": "Add a geography panel: contribution and return rate by state, with the best and worst five cities called out."}, {"id": "D8", "text": "Add a discount-depth panel: average contribution per line and return rate by discount band, with the line count shown so nobody over-reads a thin band."}, {"id": "D13", "text": "Add a customer-cohort panel: first-order discount band vs 90-day repeat rate and average lifetime contribution."}, {"id": "D9", "text": "Add a category x month heatmap of contribution margin %, with negative cells unmistakable at a glance."}, {"id": "D12", "text": "Add a marketplace comparison panel: fee %, shipping, return rate and contribution per order for Amazon.in, Flipkart, Own Website and Meesho."}, {"id": "D7", "text": "Add a monthly trend panel showing booked revenue and contribution together, so a viewer can see the month where they move in opposite directions."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "Add a returns panel: return rate by reason, by payment mode and by month, plus the rupee leakage each one represents."}, {"id": "D6", "text": "Build a listing-manager leaderboard showing GMV, contribution and contribution margin % side by side, so 'best' is never reduced to one number the viewer can't question."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Add a channel and ads panel: ad spend, ROAS and contribution after ad spend per Acquisition Channel."}, {"id": "D15", "text": "Make every panel on Page 2 respect a global date-range filter (Order Date), so the whole analytics page can be viewed for any period, not just the full year."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a short project write-up (any format: Word, PDF, Notion, a README) with three sections: (1) **Process Flow**: how you went from the raw export to cleaned data to SQL answers to your dashboard, including the decisions you made, especially how you resolved listing-manager and customer identity; (2) **Key Discoveries**: your top 3-5 findings, stated the way a decision-maker wants to hear them, not as code output; (3) **Future Growth Recommendations**: at least 3 concrete, data-backed recommendations for what Nivaas Home should do before next Diwali. This is the deliverable a hiring manager would actually read, so treat it that way."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S2", "text": "Test the pull-forward theory: did November and December volume dip because Diwali buyers had already bought? Compare against the other non-sale months and be honest about what the data can and cannot prove."}, {"id": "X-S5", "text": "Identify SKUs that lose money at every discount level, and recommend which to drop, reprice or bundle."}, {"id": "X-S4", "text": "Do review ratings predict returns? Find the SKUs whose reviews and return rates tell the same story, and the ones where they disagree."}]}]}$pack$::jsonb
from public.datasets where slug = 'diwali-discount-trap-fy2526'
on conflict (license_code) do nothing;

